Chris reflections on last night the  morning after how does that feel it's  it's still sinking in still thinking  just the the magnitude of what we've  achieved it just feels strange that  we've been every morning for the for the  last three weeks been waking up  preparing ourselves for what lies ahead  that day what mountains we've got to  ride up and for the first time today  just being able to lie back again and  switch off and think you know what we've  done it now we can relax how much  satisfaction do you take from that  achieving the job achieving it in such  in such a grand manner you won by a long  way yeah um  can take a lot of satisfaction from it I  mean it really has been months and  months of preparation to get ready for  for an event like the Tour de France to  come away having having won it the way  we did really is very fantastic feeling  your road to Paris started a long way  away from the heart of cycling's  homeland in Europe in Kenya when when  did this sort of the spark first get lit  for you um as a kid I was always on my  bike  never never really in terms of  competition or or anything like that but  as I always just loved riding my bike  and I think that's that's never to  pretty where where this spark started  the love for the sport and I can tell  you that I wouldn't be here today if I  wouldn't be able to get on my bike and  train five six hours a day if if I  didn't love it you never felt that being  that I'm at mountain biking in near  Nairobi was any barrier to one day  getting here well back then it I didn't  think it would be possible but um  I think it just goes to show that if you  really want something badly enough  you're gonna find a way to make it  happen you mentioned your mother in your  speech on the podium last night and you  mention inspiration how much did you  call on that during the race itself the  word the were moments when I thought  that's one of the reasons why why I'm  here that's one of the reasons why I'm  going through this when when the legs  are screaming at you to slow down and  stop and I could reflect and think well  actually I'm doing this for a bigger  reason I'm doing it from for more people  and then just for myself personally and  it's definitely helped to keep the  motivation up you're it's the first tool  window Brailsford already says it's you  can win more do you feel like you can do  this again you can win this race again  I'd love to be back I'd love to be back  and to to make this something that I  target for as long as I can during my  career I mean I I feel extremely  privileged to to being given opportunity  once to to come here and to try and win  it and yeah if I can have this  opportunity again in the future odds I'd  definitely go for it you've got the  yellow jersey there you said last night  in the podium that it would stand the  test of time what did you mean if you  look at the the history that history of  our sport and especially the the  revelations from from last year from  from a decade ago now I think it's only  fitting to to let people know that the  sport has changed that it's no longer a  sport where people are cheating to to  get ahead and this is one one yellow  jersey that that will stand the test of  time it's not going to get stripped and  yeah I mean we've we've come under a lot  of scrutiny during the race and I I  completely accept that people have been  let down in the past personally I've  been let down I have I was a big big fan  of of those races back then and it  really does make a mockery of of things  when when when titles get stripped so I  just wanted to put it out there and let  people know that this is one yellow  jersey that that is not going to get  stripped you you face those questions  you mentioned and there were did you  feel an extra burden as well as  everything else you had to contend with  this race you're carrying that the  faster you go the louder the questions  go it definitely added another another  aspect to our race something that maybe  we hadn't anticipated quite quite as  much as the impact it had on us it was  it was quite hard to deal with I mean  people basically saying that asking  questions about if we're complete fraud  in what we're doing and that makes it  pretty hard when when you know you've  spent months and months and months  training and really really working hard  to get here by virtue of what you've  achieved you're effectively the the  leader of that peloton now do you sense  that the riders in that peloton have  changed in their cycling has moved on  it's not just about what you and teams  can attack  definitely definitely there's definitely  a mood in the peloton that it just it's  not going to be accepted now and you can  even see guys from the past you have  served suspensions and and bans there  they're definitely not popular at the  moment and okay I can only see the sport  carrying on in this manner and going  going forward in that regard and tell me  how does after over two thousand miles  and three weeks in the settle how do you  relax you have a rest or you probably  may be racing good question oh I will  relax but I'm gonna carry on in Belgium  and Holland and do a few smaller lower  key circuit races criteriums for the  next ten days or so  so not quite the relax the relaxing that  most people would have in mind but still  a bit of fun  you'll start that way this evening this  evening
 

